we're going to begin this hour with the  continuing concerns about the impact of  this pandemic on the mental health and  academic performance of students we're  all thinking about that a survey by  mackenzie and company found that by the  end of school year last year students  between kindergarten through 12th grade  or on average think about this five  months behind in math and four months  behind on reading joining us now is  education secretary miguel cardona for  an interview you'll see only on cbs  mornings good morning mr secretary it's  nice to see you in person we only see  you from here oh i know good morning  great you're a real boy it's nice to  have you here at the table but let's  pick up that uh pick up about students  mental health what needs to be done what  can be done what should be done about  students mental health parents very  concerned about it they are concerned  all ages they are concerned a lot more  has to be done right uh we you know at  the department we we put out first of  its kind  guidance last year on how to support  students as they come back in uh you  know the american rescue plan provide  130 billion dollars and we really  prioritize ensuring that students and  families have access to better mental  health supports we've seen examples of  that across the country i have to say  educators are continue to impress me  with what they're doing whether it's  providing mental health supports in  schools after school programming but i  think it's critical that in this next  phase of recovery where we are you know  our schools are open  as we're talking about recovery we have  to make sure that the foundation of this  recovery is the mental health supports  that our students need  yeah i mean i saw a great story this  morning about a teacher um who just  takes her kids one by one and walks them  around the building and allows them to  talk  can we go to student loans we've had  many many older students reach out to us  about forgiving student loan debt and i  think that student loan debt uh  americans owe nearly 1.6 trillion  dollars in student loans there has been  talk about forgiving student loan debt  where does the biden administration  stand on that now yeah we're definitely  since day one making sure that we put  our borrowers and our students at the  center of the conversations in just one  year um president biden has forgiven  over 17 billion dollars already but  couldn't he take action now and forgive  it you know we're having those  conversations now um it's certainly  something that where we have the ability  to address uh where colleges might have  taken advantage or  loan services have taken advantage of  borrowers we're releasing and  discharging those debts and we're going  to continue the conversations about over  overall broad debt relief but i don't  want to uh i i think the culture in our  administration and in the department is  one of putting students first that's why  the public service loan forgiveness  program which was set up over 10 years  ago didn't work and in one year we got  it going we got over 700 000 people  further along in relief uh than they  were before so we're proud of that yeah  senator elizabeth warren was here and  she said the biden administration has  the power to with a stroke of a pen  erase federal student loans it sounds  like you want to do that but you don't  want to get ahead of your ball no i mean  the reality is it's it's uh we also want  to make sure that our colleagues on the  hill uh continue to fight for for that  as well  and  we know in the administration we have a  responsibility to put our students first  we think the higher education system  needs work uh we think the loan process  needs work we're  working on it so to make sure that the  system doesn't go back to where we were  too so  uh in five years from now we don't want  to be in the same position we're in so  loan forgiveness is one thing but fixing  the broken system is something that  we've been working on as well mr  secretary you've been very outspoken  about florida's don't say gay bill you  called it hateful um does the department  of education plan to take any action  against this law and i know you've  spoken to some parents about it what did  they tell you you know yesterday i had a  moving  very emotional conversation with  students parents educators from florida  and i think the words of the students uh  are most impactful i had a 12th grader  who shared that um  he knew he was different since he was  five he said this is pure hatred uh i  had a parent saying  please don't use my children as a pawn  in your political games so for me this  is about protecting all students i i  feel frustrated you know across the  world we see  we know we don't like bullies right  but yet right in our own backyard it's  happening there are over 12 states that  are passing or trying to pass  legislation like this we need to protect  our students we need to protect our  students including our lgbtq students  what can you do and educators well what  we're doing is we're lifting up their  voices we're ensuring that uh the office  of civil rights is is ready if  complaints come forward in the  implementation of this but we're sending  the message that it's unacceptable to to  bully marginalized students already we  talk about mental health right you know  students who are gay or transgender um  uh are need and need more mental health  supports  and it's really important that while  we're providing those supports we're  also not sending them to an environment  where they're feeling marginalized or  they can't be who they are dale  mentioned teachers teachers are  something else we need my mom's a  retired school teacher my aunt's a  retired school teacher my brother's a  teacher everybody everybody here in the  family and remembers teachers who've  impacted their right and i've met  teachers who have left the profession  because they can't cut it in terms of  the pay it just doesn't work for them  and we've heard so many teachers left  during the pandemic there's a major hole  in the public education system what do  you say to young people today thinking  about that career how do you get good  people into the profession you know this  is the most impactful look all of you  said with smiles on your faces yeah this  person's a teacher yeah listen we're  here because of a teacher  and it's our responsibility to lift the  profession and as secretary of education  i'm taking that very seriously we need  to increase uh teacher salary to make it  competitive uh we need to make sure that  the working conditions are conducive to  to growth professional growth personal  growth uh the days of teachers missing  three to four hours of their own family  time to correct papers we need to  revisit that the days of teachers paying  out of their own pocket money for basic  supplies second jobs but not only that  their working conditions um but voice as  we're reopening and reimagining schools  let's make sure our teachers are at the  table to talk about what schools should  look like so i'm really excited about  where we're going with education i to  the young folks to answer your question  get in the professions is the best time  to become a teacher so that's my line  thank you so much really nice to have  you here thank you thank you don't be a  stranger

